/// The router component's world.
///
/// A router is a stepped device (`wasmnet:link/stepped-device`: `ports`, `receive`, `poll`,
/// `link-state`; config through `wasi:config/store`; events through `wasmnet:link/log`) that also
/// exports `wasmnet:link/counters`, so tests can read its per-port and forwarding counters after a
/// run, and `wasmnet:control/commands`, so its static routes can change while it runs. It needs
/// no clock and no randomness: time arrives as `now` arguments, and its next deadline (an
/// ARP/NDP retry) is `poll`'s answer.
///
/// # Commands
///
/// `wasmnet:control/commands.invoke(now, command, argument)` takes these commands. A route is
/// written in the configuration's own syntax (`routes`: `10.0.3.0/24 via 10.0.2.2`,
/// `default via 10.0.2.2`, `fd00:3::/64 via fe80::2 port 1`, `10.9.0.0/16 port 1`), checked
/// against the router's ports exactly as at start. The table holds one route per prefix.
///
/// - `route-add <route>`: adds a static route. `ok("added")`; `ok("unchanged")` when that exact
///   route is there already; an error when another route holds its prefix (use
///   `route-replace`).
/// - `route-replace <route>`: adds the route, or replaces the static route for its prefix.
///   `ok("added")`, `ok("replaced")` or `ok("unchanged")`.
/// - `route-remove <prefix>` or `route-remove <route>`: removes the static route for the
///   prefix (`0.0.0.0/0` or `::/0` for a default route); given a whole route, only if it is
///   that route. `ok("removed")`; `ok("absent")` when there is none.
/// - `routes` (no argument): the table, one route per line in table order (connected routes
///   first, then static routes in the order they were added; a replaced route keeps its place),
///   as `<prefix> [via <next-hop>] port <n> (connected|static)`.
///
/// Every mutation is idempotent: asking for what is already true succeeds and changes nothing.
/// A connected route (a port's own prefix) can be neither added, replaced nor removed, and an
/// unknown command, a malformed route or a port that does not exist is an `err` that changes
/// nothing.
///
/// What a change touches, and what it does not:
///
/// - It applies at `now`, to every lookup from then on: the next packet forwarded or
///   originated takes the new route. A `poll(now)` follows the call, as after every command.
/// - Packets already waiting for a neighbour's ARP or NDP answer stay queued for that neighbour
///   and leave when it answers (or draw a host unreachable when it never does), as in a kernel,
///   where the neighbour queue comes after routing.
/// - The neighbour cache, the ports and their addresses, the links (their names, carriers and
///   captures) and the counters are untouched: a new next hop that is already known is used at
///   once, without an ARP request or Neighbor Solicitation.
/// - Each change is journalled: `route-added {prefix, via, port}` and `route-removed {prefix,
///   via, port}` (`via` is `on-link` for a route without a next hop); a replacement is a
///   `route-removed` then a `route-added`. A change also resets the once-per-destination
///   `route-lookup` trace (`trace.route-lookup = first`), so the next lookup of each destination
///   is reported with its new route.
/// - Counters: `routes` is the table's size; `routes-added` and `routes-removed` count the
///   changes (a replacement counts in both) and are listed once a command has changed the
///   table, so a router whose routes never change keeps its counter list.
///
/// This package names that combination; it adds no interface of its own. (A world added to
/// `wasmnet:link` would change that package for every device.)
package wasmnet:router@0.1.1;

world router {
    include wasmnet:link/stepped-device@0.1.0;
    export wasmnet:link/counters@0.1.0;
    export wasmnet:control/commands@0.1.0;
}
